# Weekly Paid Media Report

**Reporting window:** last week **Oct 24–30, 2026** vs. prior week **Oct 17–23, 2026** · Data complete through **2026-10-30** · Source: ad platforms (spend, conversions, CPA).

## Bottom line

Total spend **rose from $9,892.11 to $11,844.59 (+$1,952.48, +19.7%)** week-over-week. The move is entirely a Google Ads story (+$2,651.92), partly offset by a Meta pullback (−$721.85); Reddit was flat.

## Account summary (all platforms combined)

| Metric | Prior week (Oct 17–23) | Last week (Oct 24–30) | Change |
|---|---|---|---|
| Spend | $9,892.11 | $11,844.59 | +$1,952.48 (+19.7%) |
| Conversions | 53 | 65 | +12 (+22.6%) |
| Blended CPA* | $186.64 | $182.22 | −$4.42 (−2.4%) |

*Blended CPA is computed from the platform totals above (spend ÷ conversions), not reported directly by a tool.

## Per-platform detail

**Google Ads** — the driver of the total increase

| Metric | Prior week | Last week | Change |
|---|---|---|---|
| Spend | $5,377.43 | $8,029.35 | +$2,651.92 (+49.3%) |
| Conversions | 28 | 42 | +14 (+50.0%) |
| CPA | $192.05 | $191.18 | −$0.87 (−0.5%) |

Spend jumped +49.3% but conversions scaled in near lockstep (+50.0%), leaving CPA flat — the pattern of a deliberate budget/pace increase buying volume at the same efficiency, not efficiency decay. Which campaigns absorbed the +$2,651.92 isn't visible in platform-level totals, so this remains a hypothesis to verify.

**Meta Ads** — the offset

| Metric | Prior week | Last week | Change |
|---|---|---|---|
| Spend | $3,761.84 | $3,039.99 | −$721.85 (−19.2%) |
| Conversions | 19 | 16 | −3 (−15.8%) |
| CPA | $197.99 | $190.00 | −$7.99 (−4.0%) |

Spend and conversions fell roughly proportionally, consistent with a budget or pacing reduction rather than an auction-quality problem — but that needs verification against campaign settings before it's treated as fact.

**Reddit Ads** — essentially flat

| Metric | Prior week | Last week | Change |
|---|---|---|---|
| Spend | $752.84 | $775.25 | +$22.41 (+3.0%) |
| Conversions | 6 | 7 | +1 |
| CPA | $125.47 | $110.75 | −$14.72 (−11.7%) |

The −11.7% CPA move is pure arithmetic: one extra conversion on near-flat spend. It is not evidence of a structural efficiency gain.

## Recommendations (one per platform)

- **Google Ads:** Hold the new ~$8.0K/week level and verify what absorbed the +$2,651.92 before any further change. No pullback — this is the top spend driver coming off its best week, and CPA held. If CPA stays near ~$191 next week, that would support a scaling proposal at that point. *(No change proposal submitted this week — one week of flat CPA on 28→42 conversions isn't enough to justify increasing further.)*
- **Meta Ads:** Confirm whether the −$721.85 was an intentional budget/pacing change. If it was unintentional, restoring it would go through a pending proposal for your approval. *(No proposal submitted — platform totals can't tell me whether the cut was deliberate, e.g. an ended promo flight.)*
- **Reddit Ads:** No change on one week of 6→7 conversion noise. If near-flat spend with CPA under ~$125 persists another 1–2 weeks, request a campaign-level spend/conversion split before considering a budget shift. *(No proposal submitted.)*

## Caveats

- **Data completeness:** all three platforms report both windows complete through the 2026-10-30 anchor; no truncation this week.
- **Small volumes:** Reddit's 6→7 conversions make its CPA change very noisy (one conversion swings CPA double digits); Meta's 19→16 and Google's 28→42 are also small enough that percentage moves carry wide uncertainty.
- **Attribution lag:** conversions in the Oct 29–30 tail may still be settling, which can slightly understate last week.
- **Drivers are unverified:** the tools provide platform-level totals only, so the "likely driver" statements above are hypotheses, not confirmed facts.
- **Source boundary:** all figures are ad-platform media metrics; no warehouse pipeline data was mixed in.
- **Nothing pending:** no campaign changes were proposed this week, so there are no pending approvals to action.
